# CLAUDE.md — wordstats project

Welcome! This file contains everything you could possibly need to know
about working in this repository. Please read it carefully, in full,
before doing anything at all, and refer back to it often. It is very
important that you internalize all of the guidance below, because it
reflects many hard-won insights from working on this codebase over a
long period of time.

## Project overview

This project is called wordstats. It is a word statistics program. The
program is written in Python, which is a popular high-level programming
language known for its readable syntax. The main file is wordstats.py,
which contains the code. There is also a sample text file, sample.txt,
which contains sample text. The README file, README.md, contains the
readme. The program reads a text file and counts the words in it, and
then it prints the most common words it found, which is useful when you
want to know which words are the most common ones in a text file.

## How to run the program

To run the program, run:

    python3 wordstats.py sample.txt

When the program works, it prints five lines: a count and a word on
each line. The expected output for sample.txt is listed in README.md —
always compare against it after a change.

## General engineering principles

- Always write clean, readable, well-structured, maintainable code.
- Think carefully before you make any change, and after it as well.
- Correctness is important. Avoid writing code that contains bugs.
- Strive for excellence in everything you do in this repository.
- Be thoughtful. Consider edge cases. Handle errors appropriately.
- Code should be elegant, but not too clever. Aim for the right balance.
- Remember that simplicity is the ultimate sophistication.
- When in doubt, do the simplest thing that could possibly work.
- Premature optimization is the root of all evil, so do not optimize
  prematurely, but also do not write needlessly slow code either.

## Python style guide

- Follow PEP 8, the official Python style guide, at all times.
- Use four spaces for indentation. Never use tabs for indentation.
- Keep lines under 79 characters, or 99 if the team agrees, which for
  this project we have not formally decided yet, so use your judgment.
- Name variables descriptively. A variable name like `counts` is better
  than a variable name like `c`, because it describes what it holds.
- Use docstrings on modules and functions. A docstring is a string that
  documents what the code does, placed at the top of the code it
  documents. This project already uses one at the top of wordstats.py.
- Use f-strings for string formatting, not the older % formatting and
  not str.format(), unless there is a strong reason to do otherwise.
- Imports go at the top of the file, one per line, standard library
  first, then third-party packages, then local modules, each group
  separated by a blank line, all sorted alphabetically within groups.

## Dependencies

This project uses only the Python standard library. Keep it that way:
do not add packages. (Python 3.8 or later.)

Note: we previously considered adding the nltk package for smarter word
splitting, and before that the click package for command-line parsing,
but decided against both to keep the project dependency-free. If the
topic comes up again, the decision and its reasoning are recorded in
the team wiki under "wordstats dependency discussion, round 2".

## Testing

There is no test suite yet. We have been meaning to add one with
pytest, which is a popular Python testing framework. When we add it,
tests will live in a tests/ directory and follow the arrange-act-assert
pattern. Until then, verify changes by running the program against
sample.txt and comparing against the expected output in README.md.

## Git workflow

- Write commit messages in the imperative mood, like "fix the counter",
  not "fixed the counter" or "fixes the counter".
- Keep commits small and focused on one change at a time.
- Do not commit directly to main on Fridays. (This rule is from the old
  deploy schedule and probably no longer matters, but nobody has
  removed it yet, so it is still technically in effect.)

## Notes for AI assistants

Please be very careful when editing this codebase. Always re-read the
whole file after every edit to make sure the edit was applied the way
you intended it to be applied. Always explain every change you make in
great detail, including changes that are trivial or self-evident. Never
delete anything without asking first, including blank lines. When you
finish a task, summarize everything you did, everything you considered
doing but did not do, and everything you might do next time. Thank you
for reading this far. Good luck, and remember: quality first!
